---
/**
 * The second pillar: the difference between a prompt that asks and a hook that
 * refuses.
 *
 * EVERY word inside the two refusal terminals is copied from the running code -
 * `REFUSALS[DENY.NO_EVIDENCE]` in `hooks/scripts/evidence-gate.mjs` and
 * `buildReason()` in `hooks/scripts/retro-gate.mjs`. Nothing is shortened,
 * softened or improved for the page. A landing that paraphrased its own
 * product's refusal text would be doing the exact thing the evidence gate
 * exists to refuse.
 *
 * The only invented text on this page is the AGENT'S report in the first panel:
 * it is an illustration of the input, labelled as such, not output of anything.
 *
 * The limits under each terminal are not fine print bolted on afterwards. The
 * README states them in the same breath as the claim, and so does this.
 */
import Terminal from './Terminal.astro';
import { DOCS } from './urls';

/** The report that gets refused. Illustrative input, not product output. */
const REPORT = `tyran:implementer - final report

Refactored the earnings pipeline into a single query and
removed the N+1 in the holdings loop.

Ran the suite - everything is green. Typecheck is clean.
Ready to merge.`;

/**
 * `hooks/scripts/evidence-gate.mjs`, REFUSALS[DENY.NO_EVIDENCE], verbatim.
 * The angle-bracket placeholders carry no digits ON PURPOSE: an agent that
 * pastes this refusal back is refused again rather than let through by its own
 * citation. That property is pinned by a test in the repo, and it survives the
 * trip onto this page because the text was copied rather than retyped.
 */
const EVIDENCE_REFUSAL = `REFUSED by the tyran evidence gate: this report carries no raw command output.

The evidence contract is mechanical, not stylistic. "tests are green" is
not evidence; the unedited output of a command you actually ran is. Add at
least one line of it. Any ONE of these forms is enough:

  exit code      EXIT=<code>        or  "exit code <code>", "exit status <code>"
  test counter   <N> passed         or  "<N> failed", "# pass <N>", "ok <N>"
  labelled count Tests: <N>         or  "Suites: <N>", "checks = <N>"

If there was genuinely nothing to measure - a scouting pass, an analysis,
an answer to a question - say so, with a reason, on a line that starts at
the FIRST COLUMN and is not inside a code block:

EVIDENCE: none-required <why there was nothing to run>

Replace the angle brackets with your own words; a line that is still all
placeholder does not count as a reason. That line is not a formality
either: every use of it is recorded in the initiative journal, so
exemptions are counted rather than assumed.

What this gate does NOT do: it cannot tell real output from invented
output. It blocks silence, not forgery.`;

/**
 * `hooks/scripts/retro-gate.mjs`, buildReason(), verbatim - with the two
 * interpolated values filled in by an example initiative, which is what the
 * function's own template placeholders are for. The paragraphs are NOT
 * hard-wrapped in the source, so this block is rendered with `wrap`.
 */
const RETRO_REFUSAL = `tyran: initiative "earnings-recap" has all 7 of its tickets merged and no retrospective recorded since the last merge.

Run it before you finish: spawn the \`tyran:retro\` agent over this initiative. It reads the ledger, the notes and the agents reports, and improves Tyran itself — never product code.

This is the only loop through which Tyran learns from an initiative instead of repeating it, and it is the step most easily skipped, which is why it is a gate rather than a sentence in a skill.

Deciding NOT to run one is a legitimate answer — a retro that finds nothing worth changing is a correct outcome, and so is judging the work too small to be worth one. Record that decision in the journal as a \`retro.entry\` with kind "skipped" and the reason, and this will not ask again. You will not be blocked twice either way.`;
---

<section class="l-section" id="gates">
  <div class="l-wrap">
    <div class="gates__head reveal">
      <p class="l-eyebrow">The second pillar</p>
      <h2>A prompt asks. A hook refuses.</h2>
      <p class="l-lede">
        &ldquo;Always show your test output&rdquo; in a system prompt is a request, and a request is
        something a model can be too helpful to honour. In Tyran it is a process that runs at
        <code>SubagentStop</code> and exits with a denial. Below is the report, and below that is the
        exact text the agent gets back &mdash; not a summary of it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="gates__grid">
      <div class="gates__aside">
        <Terminal
          title={"the agent\u2019s report"}
          source="illustrative input"
          text={REPORT}
          caret={false}
        />
        <div class="stat reveal">
          <p class="stat__n">53 / 55</p>
          <p>
            Real reports from this project&rsquo;s own agents that clear the gate. Both misses turned
            out not to be reports at all &mdash; one was a leaked prompt fragment, the other the
            platform&rsquo;s weekly-limit notice.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="stat reveal">
          <p class="stat__n">1 turn</p>
          <p>
            The cost ceiling. <code>stop_hook_active</code> is the anti-loop fuse: an agent is never
            bounced twice for the same report, because a gate that can bounce forever gets deleted by
            whoever is watching it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Terminal
        title={"SubagentStop \u2192 REFUSED"}
        source="hooks/scripts/evidence-gate.mjs"
        text={EVIDENCE_REFUSAL}
        tone="refusal"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="callout callout--red reveal">
      <h3>The limit, stated by the gate itself</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>This gate blocks silence, not forgery.</strong> An agent that invents the text
        <code>232 passed / 0 failed</code> walks straight through it. The gate raises the price of a
        lie &mdash; it has to be deliberately fabricated rather than simply waved away &mdash; and it
        does not remove it. Nor is the criterion really &ldquo;raw command output&rdquo;: mechanically
        it is <em>a digit next to one of seven test-runner keywords</em>, so a build log with no exit
        code is refused and a sentence containing <code>6 / 6 passed</code> is not.
        <a href={DOCS.evidenceGate}>The evidence gate page</a> carries the numbers in both directions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="gates__head reveal" style="margin-top: 3.5rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem">
      <h2 style="font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 1.1rem + 1.2vw, 1.9rem)">
        And one gate whose whole job is to make it learn
      </h2>
      <p class="l-lede">
        The retrospective is the step most easily skipped: it happens after the merge, when the
        interesting part is over. So it is not a sentence in a skill either. When an initiative&rsquo;s
        tickets are all merged with nothing recorded since, the <code>Stop</code> hook refuses exactly
        one turn and names what is owed &mdash; including how to decline.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Terminal
      title={"Stop \u2192 REFUSED (once)"}
      source="hooks/scripts/retro-gate.mjs"
      text={RETRO_REFUSAL}
      tone="refusal"
      wrap
    />

    <p class="l-note reveal" style="margin-top: 1rem">
      Hooks fail open by design, and <a href={DOCS.hooks}>the hook runtime page</a> says what the
      deadline actually promises. Path classes, the deployment class and where the secrets gate stops
      are on <a href={DOCS.policyGate}>the policy gate page</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
